                <pb facs="0478" n="350" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg350"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 243.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Foulk to Mr. Bayard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Seoul, Corea</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-07-22"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 22, 1885</date>.
                        (Received September 11.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 203.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform the Department
                    of the complete withdrawal of the Chinese and Japanese troops from Seoul on the
                    17th and 20th instant, respectively, the former going to Masanpho and the latter
                    to Chemulpo. Both bodies were to sail simultaneously for their respective
                    countries on the 21st instant.</p>
                <p>With the Chinese troops a number of civilian Chinese, who had been sutlers and
                    hangers-on to the several camps, left the city. A few Japanese policemen remain
                    as a guard to the Japanese representative.</p>
                <p>The withdrawal of the troops was effected quietly and in an orderly manner,
                    occasioning no excitement whatever.</p>
                <p>There being no police force established by the Corean Government, steps are being
                    taken to provide protection for the foreign legations and consulates, using a
                    body of Corean soldiers. The formation of a police service is also under
                    consideration.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEORGE C. FOULK,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Ensign, U. S. Navy,
                            Chargé d’Affaires ad interim.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
